# Working doctrine

These rules apply in every repository unless a section explicitly limits itself to loom work.
Repository instructions and the user's task may add narrower requirements.

## Navigate before reading

In a loom repository, use the source graph before paging through files. Start with the smallest
query that can identify the relevant symbol, call path, or source range:

```text
loom map --find-all <symbol>
loom map --outline <file>
loom map --impact <symbol|path>
loom knowledge context --query "<question>" --budget-tokens 1500
rg -n '<pattern>' <path>
sed -n '<first>,<last>p' <file>
```

`--find-all` locates definitions, `--outline` gives a file's symbols and line ranges, and
`--impact` shows what reaches a symbol or path. Use the knowledge query for project questions,
`rg` for literal text, and `sed` only for the range a lookup identified. Read a whole file only
when the task requires all of it.

Inside a worktree, graph and knowledge commands may print `warning: could not refresh ...` or
`warning: failed to refresh the context cache ...`. These warnings are expected: the command
still answers from the published base layer. Do not retry or report them as blockers. That layer
reflects the branch point and cannot see edits made during the current session; read an already
edited file directly.

Do not read `CLAUDE.md`; it instructs a different agent. Do not read `doc/loom/knowledge/` file by
file. It is a roughly 200k-token corpus queried through `loom knowledge context --query`.

## Loom stage boundaries

When a task comes from a loom stage, its worktree is the complete boundary. Follow the task brief
and write only the files it assigns. Treat every other path as read-only. Never write under
`.loom/` or the legacy `.work/`; `.loom/work/` is a symlink to orchestrator state shared with
parallel stages. Stay inside the worktree.

Never run `git` during a loom stage: no add, commit, checkout, stash, restore, or status. The
orchestrator owns version-control operations.

Do not perform verification for a loom stage. This includes full builds, test suites, linters,
formatters, and type-checkers. Never run a repeated or looping check. At most, run one
narrowly-scoped check over the files you changed, exactly once, and skip it when unsure. The
orchestrator performs integration verification.

Finish a loom task by reporting the files changed, assumptions made, and anything unresolved.

## Tools and dependencies

Prefer `rg` to `grep` and `fd` to `find`. Use `uv` for Python packages and `bun` or `bunx` for
JavaScript and TypeScript packages; do not use `pip` or `npm`.

Never hand-edit a dependency manifest. Use the ecosystem command so manifests and lockfiles stay
consistent: `cargo add`, `bun add`, `uv add`, or `go get`.

## Implementation discipline

Deliver complete code. Do not leave placeholders, stubs, `pass` bodies, empty functions, or
pseudocode. If a unit is too large to implement cleanly, decompose it into complete units.

Keep files at or below 400 lines, functions at or below 50 lines, and classes at or below 300
lines. Refactor when an edit would exceed a limit.

In loom repositories, place plans in `./doc/plans/`. Never put plans in `~/.claude/plans/` or any
`.claude/plans` path.

When authorized to commit outside a loom-orchestrated stage, use Conventional Commits:
`type(scope): description`.

## Memory and attribution

For durable loom project knowledge, use `loom memory note` and `loom memory decision`. Never use
an editor-managed auto-memory directory.

Never add AI-system attribution or AI-generated boilerplate to code, commits, documentation, or
comments.

## Writing

Write laconically by default. Start with the useful result. Avoid throat-clearing, fake candour,
contrastive “not X, but Y” constructions, and closing summaries that merely restate the preceding
paragraph. Preserve necessary caveats, failures, and unresolved details even when writing briefly.

Every fenced code block must specify a language.
